@chapter 2
@title The Regulatory Framework
@lo B1
@topic t1 | Why regulation is needed | B1
@topic t2 | The international regulatory structure | B1
@topic t3 | Ownership, control and audit | B1
@topic t4 | Corporate governance | B1
@topic t5 | Principles vs rules | B1

=== Q001
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Which body develops and issues IFRS Accounting Standards?
+ The International Accounting Standards Board (IASB) || The independent standard-setter.
- The IFRS Foundation || Oversees and funds; does not write standards.
- The IFRS Interpretations Committee || Issues interpretations.
- The IFRS Advisory Council || Gives advice.
explain: Each body in the structure has a distinct role.
mistake: Naming the Foundation as the standard-setter.
tip: IASB writes the standards.
src: Study text ch2 - structure (concept basis; original question)

=== Q002
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: What is the main role of the IFRS Interpretations Committee (IFRIC)?
+ To give authoritative guidance on accounting issues where practice is inconsistent || It interprets standards.
- To appoint IASB members || The Foundation's role.
- To audit company accounts || Auditors' role.
- To set tax rates || Governments' role.
explain: Interpretations help apply standards consistently.
mistake: Confusing IFRIC with the Advisory Council.
tip: IFRIC interprets.
src: Study text ch2 - IFRIC (concept basis; original question)

=== Q003
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Who is responsible for preparing a company's financial statements?
+ The directors || Management prepares them.
- The external auditors || They give an opinion.
- The shareholders || They receive them.
- The IASB || Sets standards only.
explain: Auditors report on whether the statements give a true and fair view.
mistake: Thinking auditors prepare the accounts.
tip: Directors prepare; auditors report.
src: Study text ch2 - responsibilities (concept basis; original question)

=== Q004
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: What is corporate governance?
+ The system by which companies are directed and controlled || Standard definition.
- The process of setting accounting standards || IASB's role.
- The calculation of corporation tax || Not governance.
- The audit of financial statements || Part of oversight, not the whole system.
explain: Governance protects shareholders and maintains market confidence.
mistake: Equating governance with audit.
tip: Directed and controlled.
src: Study text ch2 - governance (concept basis; original question)

=== Q005
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t5
status: review
q: What is the status of the UK Corporate Governance Code for listed companies?
+ "Comply or explain" - comply with its principles or explain why not || A code, not law.
- It is criminal law || That describes Sarbanes-Oxley.
- It is optional with no reporting needed || Explanation is required.
- It applies only to sole traders || Not relevant.
explain: Market pressure encourages compliance.
mistake: Treating the code as law.
tip: Comply or explain.
src: Study text ch2 - UK code (concept basis; original question)

=== Q006
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Why are financial statements regulated?
+ To make them reliable and comparable and reduce scope for manipulation || Protects users.
- To increase reported profits || Not the aim.
- To remove the need for audit || Audit is part of regulation.
- To let each company choose its own rules freely || The opposite.
explain: Regulation builds trust in reported information.
mistake: Thinking regulation helps preparers inflate results.
tip: Trust and comparability.
src: Study text ch2 - regulation (concept basis; original question)

=== Q007
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: What opinion does an external auditor give?
+ Whether the financial statements give a true and fair view (present fairly) || Not a guarantee of accuracy.
- That the statements are 100% accurate || Auditors give reasonable assurance.
- That the company will be profitable next year || Not their role.
- That the share price is correct || Not their role.
explain: Auditors must be independent and qualified.
mistake: Expecting a guarantee.
tip: True and fair.
src: Study text ch2 - audit (concept basis; original question)

=== Q008
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t5
status: review
q: How is corporate governance enforced in the United States under Sarbanes-Oxley?
+ By law, with possible criminal penalties for non-compliance || Rules-based.
- On a "comply or explain" basis || UK approach.
- Entirely voluntarily || Compulsory.
- By the IASB || Not its role.
explain: SOX is a rules-based legal regime.
mistake: Calling SOX comply or explain.
tip: SOX = law.
src: Study text ch2 - SOX (concept basis; original question)

=== Q009
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: What is the role of the IFRS Foundation?
+ Overseeing governance and funding, and appointing members of the Board || Supervisory body.
- Writing every accounting standard || IASB's role.
- Interpreting standards || IFRIC's role.
- Auditing listed companies || Not its role.
explain: The Foundation sits at the top of the structure.
mistake: Thinking it writes standards.
tip: Foundation = oversight.
src: Study text ch2 - Foundation (concept basis; original question)

=== Q010
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: In a company, what is meant by the separation of ownership and control?
+ Shareholders own the company but directors run it || Owners rely on reports to judge directors.
- Directors own all the shares || Not usual in large companies.
- Auditors control the company || Not the case.
- Employees own the company || Not the meaning.
explain: This separation creates the need for reliable reporting and audit.
mistake: Thinking owners manage day to day.
tip: Owners vs managers.
src: Study text ch2 - ownership and control (concept basis; original question)

=== Q011
type: order
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Put the stages of developing a new IFRS Standard in order.
item: Identify the subject and set up an advisory committee
item: Publish a discussion paper
item: Publish an exposure draft for public comment
item: Consider comments received
item: Issue the final standard
explain: This due process gives stakeholders a chance to comment.
mistake: Placing the exposure draft before the discussion paper.
tip: Discuss, expose, consider, issue.
src: Study text ch2 - due process (concept basis; original question)

=== Q012
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: What is the main benefit of international harmonisation through IFRS Standards?
+ Financial statements from different countries are easier to compare, supporting cross-border investment || Common language.
- Every company pays the same tax || Tax is national.
- Audits are no longer required || Still required.
- Share prices become fixed || Not related.
explain: National laws and tax rules still differ.
mistake: Overstating harmonisation.
tip: Compare across borders.
src: Study text ch2 - harmonisation (concept basis; original question)

=== Q013
type: mr
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
select: 2
q: Which TWO typically form part of a national regulatory framework for financial reporting?
+ Company law || Sets legal reporting requirements.
+ Stock exchange listing rules || Apply to listed companies.
- A company's internal sales targets || Not regulation.
- The directors' personal preferences || Not regulation.
explain: National standards and tax law also contribute.
mistake: Including internal targets.
tip: Law, standards, exchange rules, tax.
src: Study text ch2 - national frameworks (concept basis; original question)

=== Q014
type: match
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Match each body with its role.
pair: IFRS Foundation => Oversight, funding and appointments
pair: IASB => Develops and issues IFRS Standards
pair: IFRS Interpretations Committee => Issues interpretations on application issues
pair: IFRS Advisory Council => Gives strategic advice to the Board and Foundation
explain: Know which body does what.
mistake: Mixing IFRIC and the Advisory Council.
tip: Oversee, write, interpret, advise.
src: Study text ch2 - structure (concept basis; original question)

=== Q015
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: What is the role of the International Sustainability Standards Board (ISSB)?
+ To develop a global baseline of sustainability-related disclosure standards || Formed in 2021.
- To set financial accounting standards || IASB.
- To audit environmental claims || Not its role.
- To collect environmental taxes || Not its role.
explain: The ISSB sits within the IFRS Foundation.
mistake: Confusing it with the IASB.
tip: ISSB = sustainability.
src: Study text ch2 - ISSB (concept basis; original question)

=== Q016
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: Which is a typical corporate governance provision?
+ Separating the roles of chair and chief executive || Avoids concentration of power.
- Allowing the CEO to chair the audit committee || Not independent.
- Abolishing non-executive directors || The opposite.
- Directors setting their own pay without oversight || Remuneration committees prevent this.
explain: Independent non-executives and committees strengthen oversight.
mistake: Allowing one person too much power.
tip: Split the top roles.
src: Study text ch2 - governance provisions (concept basis; original question)

=== Q017
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: What is an exposure draft?
+ A draft standard published for public comment before it is finalised || Part of due process.
- The final issued standard || Comes later.
- An auditor's report || Not related.
- A company's draft accounts || Not related.
explain: Comments may lead to changes before issue.
mistake: Thinking it is final.
tip: Draft for comment.
src: Study text ch2 - due process (concept basis; original question)

=== Q018
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: What is the purpose of an audit committee of independent non-executive directors?
+ To oversee financial reporting, internal control and the relationship with external auditors || Independent oversight.
- To prepare the accounts || Directors and staff prepare them.
- To set sales prices || Not its role.
- To replace the external auditor || Not possible.
explain: Independence improves the credibility of reporting.
mistake: Thinking it prepares accounts.
tip: Oversight, not preparation.
src: Study text ch2 - committees (concept basis; original question)

=== Q019
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t5
status: review
q: Which is an advantage of a principles-based governance code?
+ Flexibility to adapt to each company's circumstances || Comply or explain.
- Criminal penalties for every breach || Rules-based feature.
- No disclosure is needed || Explanation is required.
- Every company must follow identical rules || Rules-based.
explain: Rules-based systems give certainty but less flexibility.
mistake: Mixing up the approaches.
tip: Principles = flexible.
src: Study text ch2 - principles vs rules (concept basis; original question)

=== Q020
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Why must external auditors be independent?
+ So their opinion is objective and trusted by users || Credibility.
- So they can prepare the accounts more quickly || They don't prepare them.
- So they can set the company's strategy || Not their role.
- So they can own shares in the client || That would impair independence.
explain: Shareholders rely on the auditor's objectivity.
mistake: Allowing conflicts of interest.
tip: Independent = trusted.
src: Study text ch2 - audit (concept basis; original question)

=== Q021
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Which problem led to stronger corporate governance and audit requirements?
+ Unreliable and fraudulent financial reporting by some directors || Loss of trust.
- Too many accounting standards || Not the cause.
- Too much competition between companies || Not the cause.
- Falling tax rates || Not the cause.
explain: Corporate scandals damaged market confidence.
mistake: Missing the link to scandals.
tip: Scandals drove reform.
src: Study text ch2 - background (concept basis; original question)

=== Q022
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: What is a discussion paper in the standard-setting process?
+ An early document setting out the issues and possible approaches, inviting comment || First public stage.
- The final standard || Later.
- An interpretation by IFRIC || Different document.
- A company's annual report || Not related.
explain: An exposure draft usually follows.
mistake: Confusing it with an exposure draft.
tip: Discuss before drafting.
src: Study text ch2 - due process (concept basis; original question)

=== Q023
type: mcq
level: S
cat: trap
topic: t2
status: review
q: A student writes that "the IFRS Foundation issues IFRS Standards after public consultation". What is wrong?
+ The IASB issues the standards; the Foundation oversees and funds || Wrong body.
- Nothing is wrong || The body is wrong.
- Standards are issued without consultation || There is consultation.
- IFRIC issues all standards || IFRIC interprets.
explain: Exam questions often test which body does what.
mistake: Naming the Foundation.
tip: Board writes, Foundation oversees.
src: Study text ch2 - structure (concept basis; original question)

=== Q024
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: What is the role of a remuneration committee?
+ To set directors' pay independently of the directors themselves || Avoids self-interest.
- To audit the accounts || Audit committee or auditors.
- To appoint the external auditor alone || Usually audit committee recommendation.
- To set product prices || Not its role.
explain: Typically made up of independent non-executive directors.
mistake: Confusing committees.
tip: Pay set independently.
src: Study text ch2 - committees (concept basis; original question)

=== Q025
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Which is NOT usually part of a national regulatory framework?
+ The company's internal management accounts || Internal only.
- National accounting standards || Part of it.
- Company law || Part of it.
- Tax law || Part of it.
explain: Management accounts are not regulated.
mistake: Thinking management accounts are regulated.
tip: Internal = unregulated.
src: Study text ch2 - frameworks (concept basis; original question)

=== Q026
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t5
status: review
q: What happens if a UK listed company does not follow a provision of the Corporate Governance Code?
+ It must explain why in its annual report || Comply or explain.
- Directors face automatic imprisonment || Not under the code.
- The company is delisted immediately || Not automatic.
- Nothing needs to be said || An explanation is required.
explain: Investors judge whether the explanation is acceptable.
mistake: Treating the code as law.
tip: Explain non-compliance.
src: Study text ch2 - UK code (concept basis; original question)

=== Q027
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Who sits on the IFRS Advisory Council?
+ A wide range of organisations and individuals with an interest in financial reporting || Broad advisory membership.
- Only IASB members || Not the case.
- Only auditors from one firm || Not the case.
- Only government ministers || Not the case.
explain: Its breadth helps the Board hear many views.
mistake: Thinking it is a narrow group.
tip: Broad advice.
src: Study text ch2 - Advisory Council (concept basis; original question)

=== Q028
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Why do shareholders need audited financial statements?
+ They cannot see the company's day-to-day operations, so they rely on independently checked reports to judge directors || Separation of ownership and control.
- To prepare the company's tax return || Not their role.
- Because auditors manage the company || They don't.
- To set accounting standards || Not their role.
explain: Audit adds credibility to directors' reports.
mistake: Missing the agency link.
tip: Owners can't see inside.
src: Study text ch2 - audit need (concept basis; original question)

=== Q029
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: What is the role of independent non-executive directors?
+ To bring objective judgement and challenge executive directors || Independent oversight.
- To run day-to-day operations || Executives do that.
- To prepare management accounts || Not their role.
- To act as the external auditor || Not their role.
explain: They often sit on audit and remuneration committees.
mistake: Thinking they manage operations.
tip: Challenge and oversee.
src: Study text ch2 - NEDs (concept basis; original question)

=== Q030
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: How can regulation reduce the scope for "creative accounting"?
+ By restricting the choice of accounting treatments and requiring disclosure || Fewer options to manipulate.
- By allowing any treatment the directors prefer || Increases scope.
- By banning financial statements || Not possible.
- By abolishing audits || Increases risk.
explain: Standards narrow the range of acceptable methods.
mistake: Thinking regulation adds flexibility.
tip: Fewer choices, less manipulation.
src: Study text ch2 - regulation (concept basis; original question)

=== Q031
type: mcq
level: C
cat: scenario
topic: t4
status: review
q: In a listed company, the CEO also chairs the board, sets his own pay and chooses the auditors. Which governance weakness is MOST serious?
+ Excessive concentration of power with no independent oversight || Several provisions breached.
- The company has too many committees || The opposite.
- The auditors are too independent || Not the issue.
- Shareholders have too much power || Not the issue.
explain: Separate roles and independent committees address this.
mistake: Missing the combined effect.
tip: One person, too much power.
src: Study text ch2 - governance (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q032
type: mcq
level: C
cat: scenario
topic: t2
status: review
q: Companies are applying a standard in very different ways to a new type of transaction. Which body would MOST likely issue guidance?
+ The IFRS Interpretations Committee || It addresses inconsistent application.
- The IFRS Foundation trustees || Oversight only.
- The external auditors of each company || Not standard-setters.
- The national tax authority || Not its role.
explain: Interpretations are authoritative guidance.
mistake: Naming the Foundation.
tip: Diverse practice = IFRIC.
src: Study text ch2 - IFRIC (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q033
type: mr
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t5
status: review
select: 2
q: Which TWO statements about rules-based governance (such as Sarbanes-Oxley) are correct?
+ Compliance is compulsory by law || Legal requirement.
+ Breaches can lead to legal or criminal penalties || Enforcement.
- Companies may explain non-compliance instead of complying || Principles-based.
- It is more flexible than a principles-based code || Less flexible.
explain: Principles-based codes rely on market pressure.
mistake: Mixing the two approaches.
tip: Rules = law + penalties.
src: Study text ch2 - rules (concept basis; original question)

=== Q034
type: mcq
level: C
cat: trap
topic: t3
status: review
q: A shareholder sues the auditors, claiming an unqualified audit report guaranteed that the accounts were completely accurate. What is the flaw in the claim?
+ An audit gives reasonable assurance that the accounts are true and fair, not a guarantee of complete accuracy || Not absolute assurance.
- Auditors prepare the accounts, so they are responsible for every figure || Directors prepare them.
- Audits give no assurance at all || They give reasonable assurance.
- Shareholders cannot read audit reports || Not relevant.
explain: Responsibility for the accounts lies with the directors.
mistake: Treating audit as a guarantee.
tip: Reasonable, not absolute.
src: Study text ch2 - audit (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q035
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Even with IFRS Standards, why may financial statements from two countries still differ?
+ National laws, tax rules and the judgements applied may still vary || Harmonisation is not uniformity.
- IFRS Standards are identical tax laws || Not tax laws.
- IFRS bans all choices || Some choices remain.
- All countries use the same currency || Not true.
explain: Comparability improves but is not perfect.
mistake: Assuming total uniformity.
tip: Harmonised, not identical.
src: Study text ch2 - harmonisation (concept basis; original question)

=== Q036
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Why does the IASB publish exposure drafts?
+ To obtain feedback from users, preparers and others before finalising a standard || Due process and legitimacy.
- To delay standards indefinitely || Not the aim.
- To let companies ignore standards || Not the aim.
- Because laws require every country to vote || Not the process.
explain: Consultation improves quality and acceptance.
mistake: Seeing due process as a formality.
tip: Consult before finalising.
src: Study text ch2 - due process (concept basis; original question)

=== Q037
type: match
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: Match each governance mechanism with what it addresses.
pair: Separate chair and CEO => Concentration of power at the top
pair: Remuneration committee => Directors setting their own pay
pair: Audit committee => Oversight of reporting and external audit
pair: Independent non-executive directors => Objective challenge of executives
explain: Each mechanism tackles a specific risk.
mistake: Mixing committees.
tip: Mechanism to risk.
src: Study text ch2 - governance (concept basis; original question)

=== Q038
type: mcq
level: C
cat: scenario
topic: t5
status: review
q: A small listed company decides that having a fully independent board is impractical and explains its alternative arrangements in its annual report. Under the UK approach, is this acceptable?
+ Yes - under "comply or explain", a reasoned explanation is acceptable || Flexibility.
- No - every provision is compulsory by law || That is a rules-based regime.
- No - it must delist immediately || Not required.
- Yes - but only if the IASB approves || Not the IASB's role.
explain: Shareholders judge whether the explanation is reasonable.
mistake: Applying SOX logic in the UK.
tip: Explain is allowed.
src: Study text ch2 - comply or explain (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q039
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Which BEST describes the relationship between directors, shareholders and auditors?
+ Directors prepare accounts for shareholders; auditors independently report to shareholders on them || Three-way relationship.
- Auditors prepare accounts for directors || Wrong.
- Shareholders prepare accounts for auditors || Wrong.
- Directors audit the accounts for shareholders || Wrong.
explain: The auditor's report is addressed to the shareholders.
mistake: Thinking auditors report to directors.
tip: Directors prepare; auditors report to owners.
src: Study text ch2 - relationships (concept basis; original question)

=== Q040
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: What is the MAIN aim of corporate governance from investors' point of view?
+ To protect shareholders' interests and maintain confidence in markets || Monitoring those in control.
- To maximise directors' pay || Not the aim.
- To remove the need for financial statements || Not the aim.
- To guarantee dividends || Not possible.
explain: Good governance reduces the risk of mismanagement.
mistake: Seeing governance as directors' benefit.
tip: Protect owners, keep trust.
src: Study text ch2 - governance purpose (concept basis; original question)

=== Q041
type: mcq
level: C
cat: trap
topic: t2
status: review
q: A trainee says the IFRS Advisory Council "issues interpretations of standards". What is the correct position?
+ The Advisory Council gives advice; the IFRS Interpretations Committee issues interpretations || Different bodies.
- The trainee is correct || Not its role.
- The IASB never issues anything || It issues standards.
- Interpretations are issued by auditors || Not auditors.
explain: The examining team often tests these roles.
mistake: Confusing the two bodies.
tip: Council advises; Committee interprets.
src: Study text ch2 - roles (concept basis; original question)

=== Q042
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Which user group benefits MOST directly from harmonised international standards?
+ Investors comparing companies in different countries || Cross-border comparison.
- Local tax inspectors only || Tax remains national.
- Company employees only || Less directly.
- Customers buying products || Not directly.
explain: Multinational groups also save preparation costs.
mistake: Overlooking investors.
tip: Cross-border investors.
src: Study text ch2 - harmonisation (concept basis; original question)

=== Q043
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Which sequence runs from the body with OVERSIGHT, to the standard-setter, to the body giving guidance on applying standards?
+ IFRS Foundation, IASB, IFRS Interpretations Committee || Oversee, set, interpret.
- IASB, IFRS Foundation, IFRS Interpretations Committee || The Foundation oversees the IASB.
- IFRS Interpretations Committee, IASB, IFRS Foundation || Reversed.
- IFRS Foundation, IFRS Interpretations Committee, IASB || The IASB sets standards before interpretations.
explain: The Foundation oversees the Board; IFRIC supports application of standards.
mistake: Placing IFRIC above the IASB.
tip: Oversee, set, interpret.
src: Study text ch2 - structure (concept basis; original question)

=== Q044
type: mcq
level: C
cat: scenario
topic: t3
status: review
q: A company's finance director also acts as its external auditor. Why is this unacceptable?
+ The auditor would be checking their own work, so the opinion would not be independent || Self-review threat.
- Finance directors are not qualified accountants || Not the main point.
- External audits are optional || Not for most companies.
- It makes the accounts more reliable || Less reliable.
explain: Independence is essential to audit credibility.
mistake: Thinking competence is enough.
tip: Can't audit yourself.
src: Study text ch2 - independence (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q045
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t5
status: review
q: What is a DISADVANTAGE of a rules-based governance regime?
+ Companies may focus on ticking boxes rather than the spirit of good governance || Form over substance.
- It has no legal force || It does.
- It allows explanations instead of compliance || Principles-based.
- It gives too much flexibility || It gives little.
explain: Compliance can be costly for smaller companies.
mistake: Thinking rules always improve behaviour.
tip: Box-ticking risk.
src: Study text ch2 - rules vs principles (concept basis; original question)

=== Q046
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: Why do governance codes often require directors to report on internal controls?
+ Strong controls reduce the risk of error and fraud in financial reporting || Reliability.
- To replace the external audit || Audit remains.
- To increase revenue || Not directly.
- To reduce the number of directors || Not related.
explain: SOX made internal control reporting compulsory in the US.
mistake: Seeing control reporting as optional paperwork.
tip: Good controls, reliable numbers.
src: Study text ch2 - internal control (concept basis; original question)

=== Q047
type: mr
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
select: 2
q: Which TWO statements about IFRS standard-setting are correct?
+ The IASB is independent in setting standards || Independent standard-setter.
+ Proposed standards are exposed for public comment before issue || Due process.
- The IFRS Foundation writes the technical content of each standard || The IASB does.
- Standards are issued without any consultation || Consultation is required.
explain: Independence and consultation give standards legitimacy.
mistake: Mixing roles.
tip: Independent + consultative.
src: Study text ch2 - standard-setting (concept basis; original question)

=== Q048
type: mcq
level: C
cat: scenario
topic: t1
status: review
q: An investor compares two companies in different countries and finds that one capitalises development costs while the other expenses them. What does this show?
+ Differences in accounting treatment reduce comparability, which harmonised standards aim to limit || Policy differences.
- The companies have identical results || Not necessarily.
- Regulation is unnecessary || The opposite.
- Both treatments are always wrong || Not necessarily.
explain: Users may need to adjust figures to compare.
mistake: Comparing figures without checking policies.
tip: Check the policies first.
src: Study text ch2 - comparability (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q049
type: mcq
level: F
cat: revision
topic: t2
status: review
important: 1
q: Which statement about the IFRS structure is correct?
+ The Foundation oversees, the IASB sets standards, IFRIC interprets and the Advisory Council advises || Core roles.
- The IASB oversees and the Foundation sets standards || Reversed.
- IFRIC sets standards || Interprets.
- The Advisory Council issues interpretations || Advises.
explain: The ISSB sets sustainability disclosure standards.
mistake: Mixing up roles.
tip: Oversee, set, interpret, advise.
src: Study text ch2 - structure (concept basis; original question)

=== Q050
type: mcq
level: S
cat: revision
topic: t5
status: review
important: 1
q: Which pair correctly contrasts the UK and US approaches to governance?
+ UK: comply or explain (principles); US: Sarbanes-Oxley law (rules) || Core contrast.
- UK: law with criminal penalties; US: comply or explain || Reversed.
- Both are voluntary with no reporting || Neither is.
- Both are set by the IASB || Not IASB matters.
explain: Principles give flexibility; rules give certainty.
mistake: Reversing them.
tip: UK explain; US enforce.
src: Study text ch2 - comparison (concept basis; original question)
